    [Doc] Fix A5 container device mounts (#19705)
    
    ### What this PR does / why we need it?
    
    This is the `main` branch counterpart of #19704, which targets
    `releases/v0.30.0`.
    
    It corrects the documented Docker mount configuration for A5
    (950DT/950PR) environments:
    
    - Removes `/dev/devmm_svm` from A5 container commands because this
    device is not required on A5 and an explicit `--device` entry can
    prevent the container from starting when the node is absent.
    - Updates the eight-device Kimi-K3 examples to mount the complete
    `/usr/local/Ascend/driver` directory. The previous partial mounts
    exposed the driver libraries and tools but omitted
    topology/configuration files required by multi-device communication
    initialization.
    - Keeps the existing workload-specific device counts and privilege
    settings unchanged; single-device examples remain single-device
    examples.
    
    ### Does this PR introduce _any_ user-facing change?
    
    No. This is a documentation-only correction to the container startup
    commands, so no release note fragment is required.
    
    ### How was this patch tested?
    
    - Cherry-picked the release-branch fix cleanly onto the latest upstream
    `main`.
    - Reviewed all 15 documented A5 `docker run` blocks.
    - Verified that no A5 block still mounts `/dev/devmm_svm`.
    - Verified that multi-device A5 examples mount the complete driver
    directory.
    - Ran `git diff --check` successfully.
    
    No runtime test was performed because this change only updates
    documentation and requires A5 hardware to execute the commands end to
    end.
    
    ---
    
    ### Checklist
    
    The test-related checklist items below are not applicable to this
    documentation-only change.
    
    #### Testing
    
    - [ ] **Boundary / edge-case coverage**: I reviewed whether this change
    involves
    boundary conditions (e.g. empty input, single/zero element, min/max
    values,
    off-by-one, sequence-length limits, integer overflow). Where applicable,
    I
    added unit tests covering these edge cases in `tests/ut/` or
    `tests/e2e/`.
    - [ ] **Regression test**: If this PR fixes a bug, I added a regression
    test
      that would fail without the fix.
    - [ ] **Error / failure path coverage**: I covered abnormal paths
    (exceptions,
    timeouts, out-of-range, division by zero) in addition to the happy path.
    - [ ] **Meaningful & reliable tests**: New tests assert real behavior
    and are
      not written merely to satisfy CI.
    - [ ] **Performance impact**: If this PR touches a performance-sensitive
    path,
      I attached benchmark results and added performance regression tests.
    
